  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n00Z upper air analysis and current GOES water vapor imagery \ndepicts an upper level trough across the upper Mississippi \nValley and western Great Lakes region. Embedded within the \ntrough was a closed low over northern WI and the UP of MI, as \nwell as a couple of decent vorts (one notably over eastern IA), \nwrapping cyclonically southeastward through the base of the \ntrough. This pattern will continue to support periods of light \nrain/showers and drizzle especially north of the I-80 corridor \nthis morning. The main upper trough axis is progged to develop \nsoutheastward this afternoon, while surface low pressure \ncurrently analyzed over eastern Lake Superior moves east and \ntrails a secondary, fairly-stout cold front across our forecast \narea this afternoon and evening. An increase in shower coverage \nis expected as this occurs, along with a shift to blustery \nnortherly winds and colder temperatures behind the front into \ntonight. The bulk of the rain/showers should gradually end from \nnorthwest to southeast across the CWA overnight, though strong \ncold advection down the fetch of Lake Michigan (H8 temps as low \nas -4/5C by early Friday) will set up conditions conducive \n(delta-T 15-19C, ELs near 9 kft) for lake-effect rain showers \ninto northwest IN from later tonight through Friday/Friday \nnight.\n\nBlustery north-northwest winds maintain low-level cold \nadvection across the area Friday, though winds should gradually \nease late in the day/evening. Temperatures look to be limited \nlargely to the upper 40s across much of the area, perhaps \ntagging 50 if clouds break up enough by afternoon over northern \nIL. Surface high pressure then builds into the region later \nFriday night into Saturday morning, with clearing skies and \ndiminishing winds expected to allow for freezing overnight lows \nin the upper 20s/lower 30s across much of the area except the \ncore of the Chicago urban center and northwest IN where \nclouds/warmer temps are expected downwind of the lake. No \nfrost/freeze headlines are planned, as we're now well past the \nclimatological first freeze date this fall. Dry but cool \nconditions are expected Saturday with the surface ridge \ncontinuing to drift southeast across the area, with highs around\n50 and lows again Saturday night around 30 away from the city.\n\nEnsembles are in good agreement in maintaining dry weather from\nthis weekend through early next week, with the jet stream \nfocused just to the north of the region, and an expansive area \nof surface high pressure from TX to the southern \nAppalachians/mid-Atlantic region blocking any Gulf moisture \nreturn. Models do indicate a Pacific cold frontal passage in the\nMonday night/early Tuesday timeframe, though with no \nprecipitation forecast. Temperatures are expected to run at or \nabove average (especially daytime temps) with highs in the upper\n50s/low 60s (Monday looks warmest ahead of the Pacific front) \nand lows in the 30s/40s.\n\nRatzer",
